  "final": "I added a shared export pipeline in `src/exporter.py`, and both exporter modules now call it. Their output is unchanged: for a set of tricky sample rows, the new code produced byte-for-byte the same CSV and JSON strings as the original code.\n\n**What changed**\n- **`src/exporter.py` (new):** `export_rows(rows, format)` does the shared work for both formats:\n  - It picks only `id` and `name` from each row, uses `''` for missing or `None` values, and turns everything else into a string.\n  - It reads the input only once, keeps row order and builds new dicts, so the input rows are never modified.\n  - CSV output has an `id,name` header even with no rows, LF line endings, standard quoting, and a newline after each row.\n  - JSON output uses `ensure_ascii=False` and `separators=(',', ':')`, with no trailing newline (same as before).\n  - Any format other than `'csv'` or `'json'` raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")`, including `None` and lists.\n- **`src/csv_exporter.py` / `src/json_exporter.py`:** each is now a one-line wrapper calling `export_rows(rows, \"csv\")` or `export_rows(rows, \"json\")`. Their copies of the row-cleaning and output loops are gone.\n- **`src/dispatcher.py`:** I left this unchanged. It still calls the two wrappers, so it now goes through the shared code too.\n\n**Checks**\n- `python3 checks/public.py` passes.\n- I added `checks/test_exporter.py`, which passes. It covers:\n  - commas, quotes, embedded newlines and non-ASCII text in CSV and JSON\n  - missing, `None`, `0` and `False` values, and extra keys being dropped\n  - empty input\n  - iterators and generators that can only be read once\n  - inputs staying unmodified\n  - the dispatcher and `export_rows` giving the same output\n  - `ValueError` for unsupported formats\n  - both wrappers actually calling `export_rows`\n\nOne small difference: the old code looked up each value twice (`row.get(key)`, then `row[key]`), and the new code looks it up once with `row.get(key)`. Plain dicts give the same results either way.",
